{"subjectId":"c","id":"sorting-searching","title":"Sorting & Searching","group":"Data Structures & Algorithms in C","order":46,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">The classic algorithms, implemented on plain <code>int</code> arrays. Know their complexity, stability and when to reach for the library’s <code>qsort</code> / <code>bsearch</code> instead.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Algorithm</th><th>Best</th><th>Average</th><th>Worst</th><th>Space</th><th>Stable?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Bubble sort</td><td>O(n)</td><td>O(n²)</td><td>O(n²)</td><td>O(1)</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>Selection sort</td><td>O(n²)</td><td>O(n²)</td><td>O(n²)</td><td>O(1)</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td>Insertion sort</td><td>O(n)</td><td>O(n²)</td><td>O(n²)</td><td>O(1)</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>Merge sort</td><td>O(n log n)</td><td>O(n log n)</td><td>O(n log n)</td><td>O(n)</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>Quick sort</td><td>O(n log n)</td><td>O(n log n)</td><td>O(n²)</td><td>O(log n)</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td>Heap sort</td><td>O(n log n)</td><td>O(n log n)</td><td>O(n log n)</td><td>O(1)</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td>Counting sort</td><td>O(n + k)</td><td>O(n + k)</td><td>O(n + k)</td><td>O(k)</td><td>Yes</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-sort\"></i>Simple O(n²) sorts</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n#include &lt;stdbool.h&gt;\n\nvoid swap(int *a, int *b) { int t = *a; *a = *b; *b = t; }\n\nvoid bubble_sort(int *a, int n) {\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; n - 1; i++) {\n        bool swapped = false;\n        for (int j = 0; j &lt; n - 1 - i; j++)\n            if (a[j] &gt; a[j + 1]) { swap(&amp;a[j], &amp;a[j + 1]); swapped = true; }\n        if (!swapped) break;                 /* already sorted: O(n) best case */\n    }\n}\n\nvoid selection_sort(int *a, int n) {\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; n - 1; i++) {\n        int min = i;\n        for (int j = i + 1; j &lt; n; j++) if (a[j] &lt; a[min]) min = j;\n        swap(&amp;a[i], &amp;a[min]);\n    }\n}\n\nvoid insertion_sort(int *a, int n) {\n    for (int i = 1; i &lt; n; i++) {\n        int key = a[i], j = i - 1;\n        while (j &gt;= 0 &amp;&amp; a[j] &gt; key) { a[j + 1] = a[j]; j--; }\n        a[j + 1] = key;\n    }\n}\n\nvoid print(const int *a, int n) { for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) printf(\"%d \", a[i]); printf(\"\\n\"); }\n\nint main(void) {\n    int a[] = {5, 2, 9, 1, 5, 6}, b[] = {5, 2, 9, 1, 5, 6}, c[] = {5, 2, 9, 1, 5, 6};\n    bubble_sort(a, 6); print(a, 6);\n    selection_sort(b, 6); print(b, 6);\n    insertion_sort(c, 6); print(c, 6);\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>1 2 5 5 6 9\n1 2 5 5 6 9\n1 2 5 5 6 9</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-git-merge\"></i>Merge sort and quick sort</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;\n#include &lt;string.h&gt;\n\nstatic void merge(int *a, int *tmp, int lo, int mid, int hi) {\n    int i = lo, j = mid, k = lo;\n    while (i &lt; mid &amp;&amp; j &lt; hi) tmp[k++] = a[i] &lt;= a[j] ? a[i++] : a[j++];   /* &lt;= keeps it stable */\n    while (i &lt; mid) tmp[k++] = a[i++];\n    while (j &lt; hi)  tmp[k++] = a[j++];\n    memcpy(a + lo, tmp + lo, (size_t)(hi - lo) * sizeof *a);\n}\n\nstatic void msort(int *a, int *tmp, int lo, int hi) {     /* sorts a[lo, hi) */\n    if (hi - lo &lt; 2) return;\n    int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;\n    msort(a, tmp, lo, mid);\n    msort(a, tmp, mid, hi);\n    merge(a, tmp, lo, mid, hi);\n}\n\nvoid merge_sort(int *a, int n) {\n    int *tmp = malloc((size_t)n * sizeof *tmp);\n    if (!tmp) return;\n    msort(a, tmp, 0, n);\n    free(tmp);\n}\n\nvoid quick_sort(int *a, int lo, int hi) {                 /* Lomuto partition, a[lo..hi] */\n    if (lo &gt;= hi) return;\n    int pivot = a[hi], i = lo;\n    for (int j = lo; j &lt; hi; j++)\n        if (a[j] &lt; pivot) { int t = a[i]; a[i] = a[j]; a[j] = t; i++; }\n    int t = a[i]; a[i] = a[hi]; a[hi] = t;                /* pivot to its final place */\n    quick_sort(a, lo, i - 1);\n    quick_sort(a, i + 1, hi);\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    int a[] = {38, 27, 43, 3, 9, 82, 10}, b[] = {38, 27, 43, 3, 9, 82, 10};\n    merge_sort(a, 7);\n    quick_sort(b, 0, 6);\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; 7; i++) printf(\"%d \", a[i]);\n    printf(\"\\n\");\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; 7; i++) printf(\"%d \", b[i]);\n    printf(\"\\n\");\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>3 9 10 27 38 43 82\n3 9 10 27 38 43 82</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-search\"></i>Binary search</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint binary_search(const int *a, int n, int key) {\n    int lo = 0, hi = n - 1;\n    while (lo &lt;= hi) {\n        int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;     /* not (lo + hi) / 2: that can overflow */\n        if (a[mid] == key) return mid;\n        if (a[mid] &lt; key) lo = mid + 1;\n        else hi = mid - 1;\n    }\n    return -1;\n}\n\nint lower_bound(const int *a, int n, int key) {   /* first index with a[i] &gt;= key */\n    int lo = 0, hi = n;\n    while (lo &lt; hi) {\n        int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;\n        if (a[mid] &lt; key) lo = mid + 1; else hi = mid;\n    }\n    return lo;\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    int a[] = {2, 5, 8, 8, 8, 13, 21};\n    printf(\"%d %d\\n\", binary_search(a, 7, 13), binary_search(a, 7, 4));\n    printf(\"first 8 at %d, insert 9 at %d\\n\", lower_bound(a, 7, 8), lower_bound(a, 7, 9));\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>5 -1\nfirst 8 at 2, insert 9 at 5</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>In production C, use <code>qsort</code> (see Function Pointers) and <code>bsearch</code> from <code>&lt;stdlib.h&gt;</code>. Write your own when you need stability (merge sort), a specialised key, or an interview asks for it.</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why is quicksort usually faster than merge sort in practice even though its worst case is O(n²)?</summary><div><p>It sorts in place with a tight inner loop and excellent cache locality, and choosing the pivot randomly or by median-of-three makes the O(n²) case vanishingly unlikely. Merge sort needs O(n) extra memory and more data movement. Library sorts (introsort) switch to heap sort if recursion gets too deep, guaranteeing O(n log n).</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What does it mean for a sort to be stable?</summary><div><p>Elements with equal keys keep their original relative order. It matters when sorting records by one field after another (e.g. by name, then stably by department). Merge, insertion and bubble sort are stable; quick, heap and selection sort are not. <code>qsort</code> is not guaranteed stable.</p></div></details></section>"}